//? Title: Dixon Factorisation: Smooth Squares, Actual Dependencies and Honest Search Limits
//? Subtitle: Two tiny congruences, the original four composites and measured factor-base tradeoffs
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a composite integer, we want a divisor strictly between one and the integer.
Trial division can require many unsuccessful divisibility checks before finding one.
Congruences of squares turn this search into collecting smooth residues and finding
a parity dependency. We start with 77 and a three-relation example for 69, inspect
actual exponent vectors and GCD operands, and retain the original four composites
and 4,000-candidate smoothness experiment. The canonical C++ kernel now uses dynamic
parity vectors, exact full-width modular arithmetic, checked inputs and bounded
owned evidence. Measurements distinguish smooth-relation density from a guaranteed
factor, and this deterministic teaching scan from Dixon's random-sampling theorem.
)

\toc

> A first square congruence: split 77 by hand

A nontrivial divisor f satisfies 1<f<n and n mod f=0; it need not be prime.
For n=77, begin at floor(sqrt(77))+1=9. The first square residue is
9² mod 77=4=2², smooth over base [2,3,5]. Its exponent vector (2,0,0)
has zero parity already, so one relation suffices. We get X=9 and Y=2:

\[
X^2\equiv Y^2\pmod{77},\qquad
\gcd(9-2,77)=7,\qquad\gcd(9+2,77)=11.
\]

The shared batch solver collects up to k+1 relations before elimination;
it does not stop collecting at this first square. Here its four relations
arrive after ten candidates. The first dependency it tests still selects
only relation zero. All diagram labels below are computed from that run.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dixon_77.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::dixon_factor_trace(77,3,10);
  const auto& r=t.collection.relations.front();
  const auto& c=t.dependencies.front();
  std::print("First relation: x={}, residue={}, exponents {}.\n",r.x,r.residue,r.exponents);
  std::print("Batch: {} candidates, {} relations, rank {}.\n",t.collection.scanned,t.collection.relations.size(),t.rank);
  std::print("X={}, Y={}, minus GCD={}, plus GCD={}; returned factor {}.\n",c.X,c.Y,c.gcd_minus,c.gcd_plus,t.factor);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(78,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;
  box.width=3.3; box.height=.9; box.font_size=23;
  d.node("square",{0,2},std::format("{}² mod 77 = {}",r.x,r.residue),box)
   .node("roots",{0,0},std::format("X = {}; Y = {}",c.X,c.Y),box)
   .node("minus",{-3,-2},std::format("gcd(X − Y,77) = {}",c.gcd_minus),box)
   .node("plus",{3,-2},std::format("gcd(X + Y,77) = {}",c.gcd_plus),box);
  d.edge("square","roots","even exponent")
   .edge("roots","minus",std::format("difference {}",c.X-c.Y))
   .edge("roots","plus",std::format("sum {}",c.X+c.Y))
   .text({0,3.15},"A square congruence gives two exact GCD tests",25)
   .text({0,-3.1},std::format("{} = {} × {}",t.collection.n,t.factor,t.collection.n/t.factor),25)
   .save_svg("build/dixon_77.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
First relation: x=9, residue=4, exponents [2, 0, 0].
Batch: 10 candidates, 4 relations, rank 1.
X=9, Y=2, minus GCD=7, plus GCD=11; returned factor 7.
\image(figures/dixon_77.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The actual first dependency for 77 gives roots nine and two, exposing complementary factors seven and eleven. The full batch contains four smooth relations.)
\alttext(A native branching diagram connects residue four to roots nine and two, then difference and sum GCDs seven and eleven.)

> From smooth residues to a parity dependency

Let p₁,…,pₖ be the first k primes. For consecutive x values, form the
least nonnegative square residue q=x² mod n. Ignore q=0 before trial
division. A positive residue is accepted exactly when

\[
q_i=\prod_{j=1}^k p_j^{e_{ij}}.
\]

Record full exponents E and reduce each row modulo two to form A.
A nonempty selection s∈GF(2)^R with Aᵀs=0 makes every summed exponent
even. Define

\[
X=\prod_{i:s_i=1}x_i\bmod n,\qquad
Y=\prod_j p_j^{\frac12\sum_i s_i e_{ij}}\bmod n.
\]

Multiplying the recorded congruences proves X²≡Y² mod n. Hence n divides
(X−Y)(X+Y), and either GCD may expose a factor. If X≡Y or X≡−Y, the
GCDs can be one and n. A dependency alone is insufficient for success.

For 69 and base [2,3,5], the first three accepted relations are
9²≡12=2²3, 12²≡6=2·3 and 15²≡18=2·3². Their parities
(0,1,0), (1,1,0), (1,0,0) XOR to zero. The selected full exponents
sum to (4,4,0), so Y=2²3²=36 and X=9·12·15 mod 69=33.
The difference GCD is three. The fourth collected relation is retained
but is not selected by this first tested dependency.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dixon_parity.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::dixon_factor_trace(69,3,100);
  std::print("| relation | x | square residue | exponents | parity |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<t.collection.relations.size();++i) {
    const auto& r=t.collection.relations[i];
    std::vector<unsigned> parity;
    for(auto e:r.exponents) parity.push_back(e%2);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",i,r.x,r.residue,r.exponents,parity);
  }
  const auto& c=t.dependencies.front();
  std::print("\nSelected rows {}; full sums {}; X={}, Y={}.\n",c.relation_indices,c.exponent_sums,c.X,c.Y);
  std::print("Rank {}; row XORs {}; GCDs {} and {}; factor {}.\n",t.rank,t.row_xors,c.gcd_minus,c.gcd_plus,t.factor);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(78,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;
  box.width=3; box.height=1.2; box.font_size=23;
  for(std::size_t j=0;j<c.relation_indices.size();++j) {
    const auto i=c.relation_indices[j];
    const auto& r=t.collection.relations[i];
    d.node(std::to_string(i),{3.7*j,0},std::format("x = {}; q = {}\nparity ({},{},{})",r.x,r.residue,r.exponents[0]%2,r.exponents[1]%2,r.exponents[2]%2),box);
  }
  d.text({3.7,1.3},"XOR the three selected exponent-parity rows",26)
   .text({3.7,-1.4},std::format("Full sums ({},{},{}) are even",c.exponent_sums[0],c.exponent_sums[1],c.exponent_sums[2]),25)
   .text({3.7,-2.2},std::format("X = {}; Y = {}; gcd(|X − Y|,69) = {}",c.X,c.Y,c.gcd_minus),24)
   .save_svg("build/dixon_parity.svg");
}
```
