> What elimination guarantees, and what it does not

With R rows and k columns, rank(A)≤k. Rank-nullity therefore gives at
least R−k independent dependencies when R>k. The implementation stores
one identity selection row beside every parity row. Every row swap and
XOR acts on both arrays. The invariant “current parity row equals the XOR
of selected original rows” proves that a final zero row supplies a valid
square congruence. Full exponents remain untouched for computing Y.

The solver scans at most two million candidates, collects at most k+1
relations, eliminates the complete batch, and tests its zero rows in final
row order until one succeeds. Fewer than k+1 rows may already be dependent;
a singleton zero row is eligible. If all tested dependencies are trivial,
it returns zero. It does not combine different zero rows, collect another
batch, certify primality or recursively split the returned divisor.

Let S be the candidates examined, R≤k+1 the accepted rows, and
wₚ=ceil(k/64), wᵣ=ceil(R/64). Trial division uses O(S(k+log n))
modular/division operations. All-row elimination uses
O(kR(wₚ+wᵣ)) word operations and O(kR) storage for full exponents,
in addition to packed rows. These are operation bounds for this bounded
64-bit implementation, not bit-complexity claims for arbitrary integers.
Dixon's paper studies random candidate sequences and an expected
subexponential bound; this consecutive scan does not inherit that theorem.

> Retain the original four composites and inspect actual success

The next table reproduces all four original factors and adds scan count,
rank, row-XOR work, tested dependency count and the successful roots.
For 8051, the first dependency is trivial and the second gives 83.
For 15347, two trivial dependencies precede the successful one.
The returned 161 for 92434447 is composite (7·23), which is a valid proper
divisor; this API finds one divisor rather than a complete prime factorisation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dixon_batches.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> scanned;
  std::vector<std::string> labels;
  std::print("| n | factor | cofactor | candidates | relations | rank | XORs | tested dependencies | X | Y |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::uint64_t n:{8051ULL,15347ULL,92434447ULL,16843009ULL}) {
    const auto t=dm::algorithms::dixon_factor_trace(n,30);
    if(!t.factor || n%t.factor || t.factor!=dm::algorithms::dixon_factor(n,30))
      throw std::runtime_error("factor mismatch");
    const auto& c=t.dependencies.back();
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",n,t.factor,n/t.factor,t.collection.scanned,t.collection.relations.size(),t.rank,t.row_xors,t.dependencies.size(),c.X,c.Y);
    scanned.push_back(std::log2(t.collection.scanned));
    labels.push_back(std::to_string(n));
  }
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::dixon_factor_trace(8051,30);
  std::print("8051 first dependency: X={}, Y={}, GCDs {} and {}.\n",t.dependencies[0].X,t.dependencies[0].Y,t.dependencies[0].gcd_minus,t.dependencies[0].gcd_plus);
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(scanned,labels,"candidates",{37,99,235});
  p.title("Same 31-relation target; different scan lengths")
   .x_label("original composite (base of 30 primes)").y_label("log2(candidates examined)")
   .y_limits(0,15).size(1100,570).save_svg("build/dixon_batches.svg");
}
```
