> Measure smoothness over the original 4,000-candidate window

The original collector starts at floor(sqrt(92434447))+1=9615 and
uses the first thirty primes, ending at 113. It accepts exactly 25 residues
in 4,000 candidates: 0.625 percent. That count is below the solver's
31-relation target; the full solver reaches 31 after 6,049 candidates.
The fixed-window collection API deliberately keeps scanning after k+1
relations so density experiments do not stop early.

We repeat the same window for nested bases of 10,20,30,40,64 and 96
primes. Every residue smooth over a smaller base remains smooth over a
larger one, proving monotonicity of the accepted count for this fixed
window. A larger base also requires more trial divisions, more parity
columns and a larger target; a density increase alone does not prove a
shorter total factorisation. Counts in each half-window illustrate that a
finite deterministic sequence need not have a constant local density.
The accumulation curves count actual stored offsets at every 100-candidate
checkpoint; no independent report-local smoothness algorithm is used.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dixon_density.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> colors{{71,85,105},{220,38,38},{124,58,237},{22,163,74},{2,132,199},{217,119,6}};
  auto cumulative=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  auto fraction=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> bases,percent;
  std::size_t series=0,previous=0;
  std::print("| base size | largest prime | smooth / 4000 | percent | first 2000 | last 2000 |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(int k:{10,20,30,40,64,96}) {
    const auto c=dm::algorithms::dixon_collect(92434447,k,4000);
    if(c.relations.size()<previous) throw std::runtime_error("nested-base monotonicity failed");
    previous=c.relations.size();
    const auto first=std::ranges::count_if(c.relations,[](const auto& r){return r.offset<2000;});
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.3f} | {} | {} |\n",k,c.base.back(),c.relations.size(),100.0*c.relations.size()/c.scanned,first,c.relations.size()-first);
    std::vector<double> x,y;
    for(std::size_t checkpoint=0;checkpoint<=4000;checkpoint+=100) {
      x.push_back(checkpoint);
      y.push_back(std::ranges::count_if(c.relations,[checkpoint](const auto& r){return r.offset<checkpoint;}));
    }
    cumulative.line(x,y,std::format("k={}",k),colors[series++]);
    bases.push_back(k); percent.push_back(100.0*c.relations.size()/c.scanned);
  }
  cumulative.title("Actual cumulative smooth residues").x_label("candidates scanned").y_label("accepted residues").y_limits(0,330);
  fraction.line(bases,percent,"fraction",{124,58,237})
   .points(bases,percent,"",{124,58,237},5)
   .title("Nested bases retain every earlier relation").x_label("number of base primes").y_label("accepted percent").y_limits(0,7.5);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(cumulative).add(fraction)
   .size(1100,550).title("The original 4000 candidates: density changes with the factor base")
   .fit_panels().save_svg("build/dixon_density.svg");
}
```
