// result_end
\caption(Dynamic prime and relation vectors safely cross the old 64-bit boundary. A larger nullspace guarantees dependencies, while successful factoring still depends on their arithmetic.)
\alttext(Blue collected-row counts grow as base size plus one through 130 rows; red ranks remain small for modulus 77. Printed controls show cap enforcement and bounded-search failure.)

> Verification and practical scope

Native tests compare 9,568 combinations of modulus 2…300, base size 1…8
and budgets 0,1,7,60 against an independent consecutive-residue collector
and Boolean-matrix elimination. Additional fixed-window comparisons cover
714 modulus/base pairs. Each trace checks selected original rows, even
full sums, modular roots, both GCDs, rank, whole-row XOR counts and logical
capture accounting. Controls exercise prime, even, square and zero-residue
inputs; full-width modular products and square roots; high parity bits and
selection rows around 63/64/65 and 127/128/129; exact and one-short caps;
invalid inputs, scan-endpoint overflow and owned copies. The original
four factors and 25-relation density are retained.

This is a readable 64-bit square-relation method, not an arbitrary-precision
factoring service. The measured density curves and operation counts support
the bounded examples. They do not show stationary independent sampling,
a guaranteed factor per dependency, a proof of primality after failure,
or a subexponential bound for the sequential implementation.

> References

[Dixon's original paper](https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~cs812-1/dixon.pdf),
J. D. Dixon (1981), “Asymptotically Fast Factorization of Integers”,
Mathematics of Computation 36(153),255–260,
[doi:10.1090/S0025-5718-1981-0595059-1](https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-1981-0595059-1),
provides the square-relation/GF(2) construction and analyses random lists.
The input ordering, one-batch stopping rule, exact integer arithmetic,
dynamic storage and capture policy here describe the executed datamunge
variant rather than claims about the paper's random-sampling algorithm.

